content.text:
Exactly. The `schema:` option on `agent()` is the enforcement layer — the subagent is forced to call a `StructuredOutput` tool and the return value is validated against the JSON Schema at the tool-call layer. If it doesn't match, the model retries automatically. No parsing, no hoping the LLM followed instructions.

Right now our workflow uses schemas for the mechanical agents (verify gates, plan reader) but the RED/GREEN/REFACTOR agents return free text. That's the gap — those should return structured results too so the orchestrator can make deterministic decisions:

```js
// Instead of hoping the RED agent committed...
const red = await agent(RED_PROMPT, { model: 'sonnet' })  // returns text, who knows

// ...enforce the contract:
const red = await agent(RED_PROMPT, { model: 'sonnet', schema: {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    committed: { type: 'boolean' },
    commit_sha: { type: 'string' },
    test_files: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    failure_message: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['committed', 'test_files'],
}})
if (!red.committed) return logFailure(taskId, 'RED', red.failure_message)
```

This maps directly to your JSON packet doctrine — agents output raw JSON matching the schema, the orchestrator makes decisions on structured data, and only the final synthesis step renders to markdown. The workflow engine handles the "retry on schema mismatch" loop that you'd otherwise have to build yourself.

